<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Hermon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jabal al-Sheikh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Hermon is a mountain cluster on the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, its highest summit reaching about 2,814 metres. Snow-capped for much of the year, it feeds the sources of the Jordan River and was regarded as sacred in antiquity, with many temples on its slopes. Its southern slopes lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan Heights</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-hermon.jpg" alt="Mount Hermon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Almog · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermonsnow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
